WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.040
Maddy: In this video we are going to
look at and focus on employees’ rights.

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:10.440
Phillip: Employees are defined as people
who work under an employment contract and

00:00:10.719 --> 00:00:14.809
working this way means they have some rights
that other people who work in different

00:00:14.809 --> 00:00:20.120
circumstances, don’t have, although, there
are some rights that all workers enjoy.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:24.540
Maddy: So, let’s have a look at some
of the other rights employees have in

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.279
addition to general workers’ rights.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:27.319
Sickness

00:00:27.319 --> 00:00:32.110
Phillip: Statutory sick pay is available If you’re
too sick to work, and your employer has to pay

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:35.479
you at minimum amount a week for up to 28 weeks.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:40.889
Some employers also have their own sick
pay schemes, for example, workers who

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:44.840
pass their probationary period, might be
eligible for full pay when they’re sick.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:48.609
Maddy: Statutory maternity leave for
mothers of a new baby, means they

00:00:48.609 --> 00:00:50.669
can take up to 52 weeks off work.

00:00:51.259 --> 00:00:57.199
This is divided into two blocks of 26 weeks,
called Ordinary and Additional Maternity Leave.

00:00:57.709 --> 00:01:02.439
Your employer has to let you return to your
old job at the end of Ordinary Maternity

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:07.330
Leave, or to a similar role that pays the
same and has the same terms and conditions

00:01:07.499 --> 00:01:09.229
if you take Additional Maternity leave.

00:01:10.659 --> 00:01:15.070
As well as maternity leave an
employee gets Statutory maternity pay.

00:01:15.789 --> 00:01:20.350
This means that your employer has to pay you
90% of your average weekly earnings before

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:26.240
tax, for the first 6 weeks, then a minimum
amount 90% of your average weekly earnings

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.370
[whichever is lower] for the next 33 weeks.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.449
Phillip: Statutory paternity leave
and pay is also available for fathers.

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:40.539
As a father of a new baby, you’re eligible
to one or two weeks of paid leave and the pay

00:01:40.539 --> 00:01:46.700
you’ll receive is a minimum amount or 90% of your
average weekly earnings [whichever is lower].

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.980
For those parents who are
adopting there are similar rules.

00:01:52.189 --> 00:01:58.039
With Statutory adoption leave, one partner can
take 52 weeks leave, divided in the same was

00:01:58.169 --> 00:02:03.369
as maternity leave, while the other partner
can take the two weeks paternity leave, as

00:02:03.409 --> 00:02:08.680
already described and Statutory Adoption
Pay works the same way as maternity pay.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:13.610
Maddy: Shared parental leave and pay is
for when new parents including through

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:18.610
adoption, fostering, or surrogacy and who
want to share their statutory leave and pay.

00:02:19.299 --> 00:02:25.070
You can share up to 50 weeks of leave and
37 weeks of pay and the pay is the same as

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:27.160
it is for other forms of statutory leave.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:32.920
Up to date figures for pay can be found on
the government website so click on the link

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:34.860
below the video for further information.

00:02:35.839 --> 00:02:37.810
Phillip: Protection against unfair Dismissal

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:43.459
In certain situations, you may be able
to take legal action if you’re dismissed:

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:49.200
Unfair dismissal is when your employer
dismisses you without good reason, or

00:02:49.459 --> 00:02:53.519
if they fail to follow their own formal
disciplinary or dismissal process.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:58.940
Constructive dismissal is when you’re forced
to leave your job because of how your employer

00:02:58.979 --> 00:03:04.360
has treated you, for example if they’ve
refused to pay you or demote you for no reason.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:10.369
Employees should try and sort out with
the employer any disputes directly or

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:14.369
they can exercise their rights by going
to an employment or industrial tribunal.

00:03:15.209 --> 00:03:17.690
Maddy: You have the right
to ask for Flexible working.

00:03:18.249 --> 00:03:23.130
This means being able to move your hours,
starting and finishing times, or work

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:25.609
location to be more convenient for you.

00:03:26.149 --> 00:03:30.559
For example, working from home, or
starting early so you can finish early.

00:03:31.079 --> 00:03:35.840
However, your employer doesn’t have to meet
your requests, but they must deal with them

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.009
in a reasonable way, such as by having a
meeting with you to discuss your request.

00:03:40.849 --> 00:03:42.549
Phillip: Time off for emergencies.

00:03:43.559 --> 00:03:48.229
If an emergency has affected a dependent,
your employer must let you take time off to

00:03:48.879 --> 00:03:54.969
care for them, this may include a spouse,
parent, grandparent, child or grandchild.

00:03:55.599 --> 00:03:59.999
There’s no limit to how much time you
can take off, but your employer isn’t

00:04:00.059 --> 00:04:02.139
obliged to pay you for this time.

00:04:03.029 --> 00:04:04.750
Maddy: Statutory Redundancy Pay

00:04:05.679 --> 00:04:10.239
If you’re made redundant by your employer,
which means your employer has decided that

00:04:10.249 --> 00:04:14.829
they no longer require the job you perform and
that there’s no replacement role available for

00:04:14.829 --> 00:04:17.940
you, you’re entitled to receive redundancy pay.

00:04:18.469 --> 00:04:22.590
However, you’re only eligible for
this, if you’ve worked for your current

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:24.269
employer for at least two years.

00:04:24.929 --> 00:04:29.970
You’ll get half a weeks’ pay for each full
year you were employed under 22, or a full

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:33.730
weeks’ pay for each full year between 22-41.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:38.919
You receive one and a half weeks pay for
each full year you were 41 and older.

00:04:39.599 --> 00:04:42.249
Length of service is capped at 20 years.

